{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c6f3","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07a5e-8204-7c61-b778-7e703c0d991a"},"sequence":468,"recorded_at":1780531400000931,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"2a014316-3376-47a3-a185-8cb71122e6e6","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"2a014316-3376-47a3-a185-8cb71122e6e6","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"2a014316-3376-47a3-a185-8cb71122e6e6"},"call_id":"call_01a07a5f92317612baa82c2bbb2b5ded","text":"Read text file `internal/terminalbackend/refusal_arm_inventory_test.go`.\n1|// AST-derived refusal-arm inventory for the terminal backend packages.\n2|//\n3|// The failure this file exists to prevent: a brand-new production refusal\n4|// arm carrying a brand-new unasserted detail shipped while `gofmt`,\n5|// `go vet`, `go test ./...`, and `tracecheck` all stayed green. A\n6|// hand-maintained arm list cannot catch that; a derived one can, but only\n7|// if it is checked in BOTH directions. The forward direction alone passes\n8|// vacuously on a truncated derivation: every derived arm asserted is true\n9|// when the derivation silently dropped half the package. So this file\n10|// derives every refusal arm from the production AST, requires each to\n11|// resolve to exactly one declared row naming a real asserting test, and\n12|// requires every declared row to resolve to exactly one derived arm.\n13|//\n14|// Modelled on internal/canonicaljson/grammar_inventory_test.go: derivation\n15|// fails closed on an empty, short, or unparseable input, and the declared\n16|// table cannot move its own goalposts because the derivation never\n17|// consults it.\n18|//\n19|// Resolution is EXECUTED, not textual: every non-bound row carries a\n20|// witness executed through a public production entry point by\n21|// TestEveryDeclaredArmRefusesAtItsEntry (owns its proof in\n22|// refusal_arm_witnesses_test.go), which requires the exact wire code at\n23|// the exact static detail. A row that merely mentions the detail in some\n24|// test's source proves nothing and resolves nothing.\n25|//\n26|// A pair prove alone does NOT attribute the arm: 62 of the 184 witnessed\n27|// arms share their (code, detail) clause with a sibling, so a widened\n28|// earlier guard swallows a later arm's inputs and the later witness\n29|// still passes through the sibling (P8: CheckEntrypoint's first session\n30|// parse widened to also compare, killing the second parse and the\n31|// comparison arms, left every witness green). Attribution is the pair\n32|// prove PLUS the exercised-site audit in refusal_site_audit_test.go:\n33|// every production refusal construction records its file:line at\n34|// runtime, the post-run audit requires every derived non-bound line to\n35|// have fired, and TestDerivedSiteLinesAreExactlyRowed maps every row to\n36|// exactly one derived line. A dead-by-construction arm never fires, so\n37|// it reddens in the audit even though its pair still refuses through its\n38|// sibling. Live example of the bound alternative: CheckTransition's\n39|// \"operation vocabulary\" arm (conformance.go) is unreachable —\n40|// CheckTransition parses through ParseOperation first and\n41|// lookupTransition covers every admitted operation — so the row declares\n42|// boundUnreachableVocabulary, pinned by\n43|// TestCheckTransitionOperationVocabularyIsUnreachable, instead of\n44|// resolving through ParseOperation's sibling arm sharing the detail.\n45|// The five re-parse arms declare boundDefensiveReparse the same way, and\n46|// TestDefensiveBoundsAreExactlyThese pins the full bound set.\n47|//\n48|// Stated residual: the audit binds every ROW to a fired LINE, but not\n49|// every WITNESS to its row's line — two witnesses firing each other's\n50|// sites in complementary swap would stay green. No such swap exists\n51|// (each prove documents its pass-earlier-sites construction), and the\n52|// shape is perverse rather than adjacent, but it is disclosed, not\n53|// closed. provider and provhost share the same residual by construction:\n54|// their witnesses bind codes, their audits bind lines.\n55|//\n56|// Stated bounds (not inferred):\n57|//   - The inventory proves every arm is DECLARED and every non-bound arm\n58|//     is WITNESSED at a public entry with its exact code and detail.\n59|//     Bound arms (defensive re-parses, the unreachable vocabulary arm)\n60|//     are proved by their pinning tests, which fail closed when the\n61|//     production shape they assume changes.\n62|//   - Kill inflation: both inventory directions fail on ANY added or\n63|//     deleted production arm by construction, so an arm-deleting mutant\n64|//     elsewhere reads as killed by the inventory alone with no behavioral\n65|//     change. Mutation scores measured with this file present are NOT\n66|//     comparable to pre-inventory scores; review the failing-test list,\n67|//     not just the exit code.\n68|//   - errors.New/fmt.Errorf sites are not wire refusals and carry no arm.\n69|//     They live only in validatePlatforms (funneled into one *Error arm\n70|//     whose detail set is pinned exactly) and DigestFile (I/O errors that\n71|//     must never be *Error). Both properties are asserted below, in every\n72|//     FuncDecl body plus every package-level var initializer, and any\n73|//     reference to either constructor outside direct-call position\n74|//     (e.g. `newPlain := errors.New`) fails the suite, so an aliased\n75|//     construction cannot ship unattributed.\n76|//   - The DigestFile half of that allowlist is function-wide with no\n77|//     detail pin, unlike validatePlatforms' derived exact set: a new\n78|//     errors.New on any path inside DigestFile passes the gate (and the\n79|//     never-wire behavioral assertion, which only drives the current\n80|//     paths). Disclosed, not closed.\n81|//   - Custom error types outside the two recognised spellings (*Error,\n82|//     errors.New, fmt.Errorf) are outside both derivations: a bespoke\n83|//     error type smuggled as a refusal survives. Disclosed, not closed.\n84|//   - Constructor Details are single static literals by assertion: the\n85|//     derivation fatals on any mismatchf/integrityFailure call without\n86|//     exactly one string-literal argument, so format-verb interpolation\n87|//     cannot enter the table. Zero multi-argument sites today; go vet's\n88|//     printf analyzer independently refuses a non-constant format string.\n89|package terminalbackend\n90|\n91|import (\n92|\t\"bytes\"\n93|\t\"encoding/json\"\n94|\t\"errors\"\n95|\t\"go/ast\"\n96|\t\"go/token\"\n97|\t\"os\"\n98|\t\"path/filepath\"\n99|\t\"strconv\"\n100|\t\"strings\"\n101|\t\"testing\"\n102|\n103|\t\"github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager/internal/invcore\"\n104|\t\"github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager/internal/scalar\"\n105|)\n106|\n107|// refusalArm is one derived production refusal: a wire-code *Error\n108|// construction site keyed by file, enclosing function (receiver-qualified\n109|// for methods), code symbol, static detail, and occurrence index within\n110|// that key. The index keeps two identical arms in one function distinct:\n111|// adding a second arm with an already-declared key fails on the missing\n112|// #2 row instead of hiding behind #1.\n113|//\n114|// Line carries the production file:line of the construction call: the\n115|// refuse() call line for &Error literals, the constructor call line for\n116|// mismatchf/integrityFailure. The exercised-site audit requires every\n117|// derived line to fire at least once across the full suite run, so an\n118|// arm shadowed dead by a widened sibling guard reddens there even though\n119|// its (code, detail) pair still refuses through the sibling. Two arms\n120|// sharing one line fail the derivation: one line cannot attribute two\n121|// arms.\n122|type refusalArm struct {\n123|\tfile       string\n124|\tfunction   string\n125|\tcode       string\n126|\tdetail     string\n127|\toccurrence int\n128|\tline       int\n129|}\n130|\n131|// constructorFunctions build refusals instead of issuing them. Their own\n132|// *Error sites are the funnel, not arms.\n133|var constructorFunctions = map[string]bool{\n134|\t\"mismatchf\":        true,\n135|\t\"integrityFailure\": true,\n136|}\n137|\n138|// passthroughDetail marks the single dynamic-detail funnel: Registration\n139|// validation wraps validatePlatforms errors via err.Error(). Every other\n140|// Detail expression must be a static string literal; a new dynamic detail\n141|// fails the derivation rather than entering the table silently.\n142|const passthroughDetail = \"passthrough:err.Error()\"\n143|\n144|// deriveRefusalArms walks every production file of this package (every\n145|// *.go except *_test.go, so a new production file is scanned without\n146|// anyone remembering it) and returns every refusal arm in source order.\n147|// It fails closed: an unparseable file, a non-static code or detail, and\n148|// an empty result are all fatal, never an empty table.\n149|//\n150|// Coverage is declaration-complete, not FuncDecl-complete: every FuncDecl\n151|// body (including nested func literals, methods, and init) plus every\n152|// package-level var initializer is scanned, so a refusal arm hiding in a\n153|// package-level `var f = func() *Error {...}` is derived like any other.\n154|// Constructor references outside direct-call position (`refuse :=\n155|// mismatchf`) are rejected outright by rejectConstructorAliases below:\n156|// an aliased refusal cannot be inventoried, so it fails the suite rather\n157|// than shipping silently.\n158|func deriveRefusalArms(t *testing.T) []refusalArm {\n159|\tt.Helper()\n160|\n161|\t// Production-file selection and fail-closed parsing are the shared\n162|\t// core's: a new production file is scanned without anyone\n163|\t// remembering it, and an unparseable file fails, never skips.\n164|\tfiles, fileSet := invcore.MustScanProduction(t, \".\")\n165|\n166|\tvar arms []refusalArm\n167|\tparsed := 0\n168|\tfor _, production := range files {\n169|\t\tfile, name := production.Syntax, production.Name\n170|\t\tparsed++\n171|\t\tlineOf := func(position token.Pos) int {\n172|\t\t\treturn fileSet.Position(position).Line\n173|\t\t}\n174|\t\t// errorLiteral reads one &Error{Code:, Detail:} literal into its\n175|\t\t// static code symbol and detail. Anything else fails the\n176|\t\t// derivation: a refusal the inventory cannot name is a refusal it\n177|\t\t// cannot witness.\n178|\t\terrorLiteral := func(functionName string, literal *ast.CompositeLit) (string, string) {\n179|\t\t\tidentifier, ok := literal.Type.(*ast.Ident)\n180|\t\t\tif !ok || identifier.Name != \"Error\" {\n181|\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"%s: refuse wraps a non-Error literal; only &Error literals route through refuse\", functionName)\n182|\t\t\t}\n183|\t\t\tcode, detail := \"\", \"\"\n184|\t\t\tfor _, element := range literal.Elts {\n185|\t\t\t\tpair, ok := element.(*ast.KeyValueExpr)\n186|\t\t\t\tif !ok {\n187|\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n188|\t\t\t\t}\n189|\t\t\t\tkey, ok := pair.Key.(*ast.Ident)\n190|\t\t\t\tif !ok {\n191|\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n192|\t\t\t\t}\n193|\t\t\t\tswitch key.Name {\n194|\t\t\t\tcase \"Code\":\n195|\t\t\t\t\tsymbol, ok := pair.Value.(*ast.Ident)\n196|\t\t\t\t\tif !ok {\n197|\t\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"%s: refusal code is not a static Code symbol; \"+\n198|\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\"a computed code cannot be inventoried\", functionName)\n199|\t\t\t\t\t}\n200|\t\t\t\t\tcode = symbol.Name\n201|\t\t\t\tcase \"Detail\":\n202|\t\t\t\t\tdetail = staticDetail(t, functionName, pair.Value)\n203|\t\t\t\t}\n204|\t\t\t}\n205|\t\t\tif code == \"\" || detail == \"\" {\n206|\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"%s: refusal without a static code and detail; \"+\n207|\t\t\t\t\t\"the inventory cannot name what it cannot read\", functionName)\n208|\t\t\t}\n209|\t\t\treturn code, detail\n210|\t\t}\n211|\t\tcollect := func(functionName string, root ast.Node) {\n212|\t\t\tast.Inspect(root, func(node ast.Node) bool {\n213|\t\t\t\tswitch expression := node.(type) {\n214|\t\t\t\tcase *ast.UnaryExpr:\n215|\t\t\t\t\tif expression.Op != token.AND {\n216|\t\t\t\t\t\treturn true\n217|\t\t\t\t\t}\n218|\t\t\t\t\tliteral, ok := expression.X.(*ast.CompositeLit)\n219|\t\t\t\t\tif !ok {\n220|\t\t\t\t\t\treturn true\n221|\t\t\t\t\t}\n222|\t\t\t\t\tif identifier, ok := literal.Type.(*ast.Ident); !ok || identifier.Name != \"Error\" {\n223|\t\t\t\t\t\treturn true\n224|\t\t\t\t\t}\n225|\t\t\t\t\t// A wrapped literal never reaches here: the refuse\n226|\t\t\t\t\t// CallExpr case below consumes it and prunes the\n227|\t\t\t\t\t// descent. An &Error literal built outside refuse is\n228|\t\t\t\t\t// an unregistered construction: it refuses without\n229|\t\t\t\t\t// recording its site, so the exercised-site audit\n230|\t\t\t\t\t// cannot see it, and it fails here instead of\n231|\t\t\t\t\t// shipping silently.\n232|\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"%s (%s:%d): &Error literal outside refuse(); \"+\n233|\t\t\t\t\t\t\"route every production refusal through refuse so the site audit can attribute it\",\n234|\t\t\t\t\t\tfunctionName, name, lineOf(expression.Pos()))\n235|\t\t\t\t\treturn false\n236|\t\t\t\tcase *ast.CallExpr:\n237|\t\t\t\t\tif identifier, ok := expression.Fun.(*ast.Ident); ok && identifier.Name == \"refuse\" {\n238|\t\t\t\t\t\tif len(expression.Args) != 1 {\n239|\t\t\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"%s: refuse with %d arguments; refuse wraps exactly one &Error literal\",\n240|\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tfunctionName, len(expression.Args))\n241|\t\t\t\t\t\t}\n242|\t\t\t\t\t\tunary, ok := expression.Args[0].(*ast.UnaryExpr)\n243|\t\t\t\t\t\tif !ok || unary.Op != token.AND {\n244|\t\t\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"%s (%s:%d): refuse wraps a non-literal; refuse takes exactly one &Error literal, never a variable\",\n245|\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tfunctionName, name, lineOf(expression.Pos()))\n246|\t\t\t\t\t\t}\n247|\t\t\t\t\t\tliteral, ok := unary.X.(*ast.CompositeLit)\n248|\t\t\t\t\t\tif !ok {\n249|\t\t\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"%s (%s:%d): refuse wraps a non-literal; refuse takes exactly one &Error literal, never a variable\",\n250|\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tfunctionName, name, lineOf(expression.Pos()))\n251|\t\t\t\t\t\t}\n252|\t\t\t\t\t\tcode, detail := errorLiteral(functionName, literal)\n253|\t\t\t\t\t\tarms = append(arms, refusalArm{\n254|\t\t\t\t\t\t\tfile: name, function: functionName, code: code, detail: detail,\n255|\t\t\t\t\t\t\tline: lineOf(expression.Pos()),\n256|\t\t\t\t\t\t})\n257|\t\t\t\t\t\treturn false\n258|\t\t\t\t\t}\n259|\t\t\t\t\tidentifier, ok := expression.Fun.(*ast.Ident)\n260|\t\t\t\t\tif !ok || !constructorFunctions[identifier.Name] {\n261|\t\t\t\t\t\treturn true\n262|\t\t\t\t\t}\n263|\t\t\t\t\tif len(expression.Args) != 1 {\n264|\t\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"%s: %s with %d arguments; Detail must be a single \"+\n265|\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\"static literal and never interpolate local data, and a \"+\n266|\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\"variadic detail cannot be inventoried\",\n267|\t\t\t\t\t\t\tfunctionName, identifier.Name, len(expression.Args))\n268|\t\t\t\t\t}\n269|\t\t\t\t\tliteral, ok := expression.Args[0].(*ast.BasicLit)\n270|\t\t\t\t\tif !ok || literal.Kind != token.STRING {\n271|\t\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"%s: %s detail is not a static string literal; \"+\n272|\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\"a computed detail cannot be inventoried\", functionName, identifier.Name)\n273|\t\t\t\t\t}\n274|\t\t\t\t\tdetail, err := strconv.Unquote(literal.Value)\n275|\t\t\t\t\tif err != nil {\n276|\t\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"%s: unquote detail: %v\", functionName, err)\n277|\t\t\t\t\t}\n278|\t\t\t\t\tcode := \"CodeMismatch\"\n279|\t\t\t\t\tif identifier.Name == \"integrityFailure\" {\n280|\t\t\t\t\t\tcode = \"CodeIntegrityFailure\"\n281|\t\t\t\t\t}\n282|\t\t\t\t\tarms = append(arms, refusalArm{\n283|\t\t\t\t\t\tfile: name, function: functionName, code: code, detail: detail,\n284|\t\t\t\t\t\tline: lineOf(expression.Pos()),\n285|\t\t\t\t\t})\n286|\t\t\t\t}\n287|\t\t\t\treturn true\n288|\t\t\t})\n289|\t\t}\n290|\t\tfor _, declaration := range file.Decls {\n291|\t\t\tswitch decl := declaration.(type) {\n292|\t\t\tcase *ast.FuncDecl:\n293|\t\t\t\tif decl.Body == nil {\n294|\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n295|\t\t\t\t}\n296|\t\t\t\tif constructorFunctions[decl.Name.Name] {\n297|\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n298|\t\t\t\t}\n299|\t\t\t\tfunctionName := decl.Name.Name\n300|\t\t\t\tif decl.Recv != nil && len(decl.Recv.List) > 0 {\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
